 To evaluate the expression $2f(2) - f(8)$, we first need to define the function $f(x) = x^2 + 2\sqrt{x}$. Then, we will compute the values of $f(2)$ and $f(8)$ separately and substitute them into the expression. Specifically, we have:

1. $f(2) = 2^2 + 2\sqrt{2} = 4 + 2\sqrt{2}$
2. $f(8) = 8^2 + 2\sqrt{8} = 64 + 2 \cdot 2\sqrt{2} = 64 + 4\sqrt{2}$

So, the expression $2f(2) - f(8)$ becomes:
\[2f(2) - f(8) = 2(4 + 2\sqrt{2}) - (64 + 4\sqrt{2}) = 8 + 4\sqrt{2} - 64 - 4\sqrt{2} = 8 - 64 = -56\]

The final answer is $-56$. The code to compute this is as follows:`
\begin{align*}
f(x) = x^2 + 2\sqrt{x}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
f(2) = 4 + 2\sqrt{2}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
f(8) = 64 + 4\sqrt{2}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
2f(2) - f(8) = 2(4 + 2\sqrt{2}) - (64 + 4\sqrt{2}) = 8 + 4\sqrt{2} - 64 - 4\sqrt{2} = 8 - 64 = -56
\end{align*}

Thus, the value of $2f(2) - f(8)$ is $\boxed{-56}$.

